WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.784
An airway can reopen, and still be injured.

00:00:03.784 --> 00:00:06.968
When air moves through a liquid-filled airway, its

00:00:06.968 --> 00:00:10.153
advancing boundary may stress the epithelial cells. We

00:00:10.153 --> 00:00:12.939
wanted to test that hidden mechanical event.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.909
Here is our actual experimental apparatus, published in

00:00:23.909 --> 00:00:27.217
the paper. Rat alveolar epithelial cells were grown

00:00:27.217 --> 00:00:30.526
in a parallel-plate chamber. We moved one air

00:00:30.526 --> 00:00:33.835
bubble across the cell layer under controlled conditions.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.054
These are the study's real fluorescence micrographs. Green

00:00:44.054 --> 00:00:47.508
and red staining helped distinguish surviving from dead

00:00:47.508 --> 00:00:50.962
cells after exposure. They are measurements from cultured

00:00:50.962 --> 00:00:53.984
cells, not images from a patient's lung.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.439
The measured cell death after bubble exposure was

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:10.279
about fifty-five percent. Pretreatment with dexamethasone lowered the

00:01:10.279 --> 00:01:14.118
reported average to about fifteen percent; with trans-dehydroandrosterone,

00:01:14.118 --> 00:01:17.957
to about sixteen percent. These results are in

00:01:17.957 --> 00:01:18.437
vitro.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.397
Actin staining provided another window into the response.

00:01:28.397 --> 00:01:32.194
The treatments altered cytoskeletal organisation, suggesting that the

00:01:32.194 --> 00:01:35.990
cells' mechanical state may have influenced how they

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:37.889
tolerated the moving interface.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.189
Atomic-force microscopy provided additional evidence about cell mechanics.

00:01:47.189 --> 00:01:50.779
But a single bubble over a cell culture

00:01:50.779 --> 00:01:54.368
is not a ventilated patient. Clinical protection remains

00:01:54.368 --> 00:01:56.611
a question for future research.

